tions and that  leaves us with a lot of ambiguity  we can use those observations to  generate beliefs about the world  and those beliefs evolve over time as we  gather new information  now the world is structured and we know  that the world is structured and we  exploit some of that structure and here  that structure is manifested graphically  as having some arrows some causal arrows  for what variables influence what other  variables later in in time so you can  see that the arrows point kind of down  for these two top circles and  up from the bottom back to the top and  this leads to a kind of circulation this  is a cartoon of one causal structure  that you might see  now our brain may have a parallel  structure  in good cases it would know the  structure of the world and maybe it  makes some erroneous assumptions about  the way the world works  and our observations as we parse them by  our brain tissue is  divided into things that are relevant  for different aspects of the world that  we see and so that informs our beliefs  now the world is stochastic and that  means that there's a lot of uncertainty  about what we're actually seeing and how  the dynamics evolve that we don't get  perfect information about  and that means that our beliefs are not  just a copy of the world state  but instead should capture multiple  numbers per variable of interest and  that would be something like a best  guess plus your uncertainty about it and  there might be joint uncertainty so  there's there's more numbers that the  brain has to use has to represent in  order to capture the dynamics of the  world than just the variables it's  capturing  the world is also non-linear the  dynamics and updates of the world are  complicated and although it might be  that in some cases the brain tries to  update its world estimates  in in one step  it's easier for the brain using its  actual hardware  and the mechanisms that it has available  to update them in multiple steps using  intermediate or auxiliary variables  that are  represented in some other neural  populations or maybe in the very same  population just in different dimensions  so this is going on over time  there's also no reason that the number  of neurons that you need  to compute is the same as the number of  beliefs that you have so there might be  a distributed code with in fact there's  lots of evidence that there is  the information is distributed across  many neurons  and so the dynamics of all of these  neurons that there may not be any  beliefs explicitly anywhere but it may  be just implicit in these populations  this is the the metaphor that i like to  think about  and so  now as you're going along and updating  these belief dynamics implicitly in the  populations those are driving actions  so the the behavioral model that we use  to describe what we're thinking  is based on a belief but causally  speaking really what's going on is that  of course the neurons are driving the  actions  but  we find it very convenient and  theoretically helpful to consider that  the beliefs are driving the actions and  we just know that those beliefs are  implemented by a bunch of neurons  so our our overall hypothesis here is  that there are latent variables about  the world in fact probabilistic ones  that are distributed across neurons and  they perform implicit inference and  select actions through the nonlinear  dynamics and those nonlinear dynamics  pertain to the beliefs that we have  about the world  now  if you go back over 100 years helmholtz  proposed that our brain is performing  unconscious inference  and the implication of that and of many  others theories  is that there's a match between the  brain and the world  that's been really fruitful as a way of  generating hypotheses about the world  and generating testable predictions and  sometimes the brain is in fact very  close to optimal which is remarkable and  marvelous but many other times the brain  falls short of what we would consider to  be optimal  and we know from plenty of experience  with human foibles how true that is  so  how then do we get any guidance from  theory if the theory tells us the best  thing to do there are so many ways to be  wrong what kind of guidance could we get  well what if the agent in this case the  brain or an animal is wrong but  rational  that means doing the wrong things but  for the right reasons  have some justification for what's going  on and we can rationalize those actions  as being  right under the wrong assumptions  so we developed a framework to formalize  this idea which we call inverse rational  control  and so we assume that the agent is  optimal but under some subjective  assumptions which may not match whatever  experiment they're being measured in  then we observe the agent's actions  and the sensory observations of the  agent  and we estimate a model  theta for the dynamics the uncertainties  the rewards the action costs all the  assumptions that the animal has about  the world  along the way we have to infer the  latent beliefs  that dictate those actions  and we put all that together to come up  with a marginal likelihood  over the observations and actions that  we see given the model and we optimize  that marginal likelihood over models  in other words  we parameterize agents by the task that  they solve best  and then we solve for all possible  policies within that task family so for  each family member theta some tasks and  assumptions we come up with the policy i  for choosing the actions from a given  belief  and again given the  given the model given the assumptions we  then use that policy to compute a  likelihood over the sensory data and  that gives us a likelihood over all  tasks which we then maximize to find the  best interpretation of animal behavior  as rational that is optimal under the  assumptions of theta  and we've shown for simulated agents  that this successfully recovers false  belief so for example in this  publication um that's listed down below  we have an agent who's foraging between  a couple of boxes  choosing when to look in the box for  food and making sensory observations  about the likely quality of the box  along the way the true agent that we've  simulated has some real beliefs which  are shown in blue  and  we also are able we are also showing  here this gray grayscale in the  background which is our posterior  over those beliefs so our probability  over their probability  and you can see that there's a very nice  agreement between the inferred beliefs  and the actual beliefs of this  suboptimal behaving animal or simulated  animal  so this is very encouraging and we are  now  deeply involved in testing this in  animals in real tasks and hopefully  we'll have some cool results to report  to you in the not too distant future  now  this is all behavior and so what happens  when we start to look inside the brain  which is ultimately the goal here well  we need to have some model  of how that information all that  probabilistic information the beliefs  that the animal has about the world how  they might be manifested in neurons  we're looking for that and we want to  know what properties it has  so one possibility is that we could have  a model with a really clear  correspondence between the factors in  the world and the factors in the brain  groups of neurons would then represent  individual aspects that are driving  these variables or causing those  variables to interact so the circles  here represent variables the squares  represent evidence about those variables  or interactions between those variables  and we have a model that says  how these things should interact if  you're implementing them by population  codes but this right here is really like  a grandmother cell representation  and so it may well be possible or i  would say likely that all of those  things are mushed together into a big  population of over neurons with  overlapping tuning mixed selectivity and  there's a lot of evidence for this  although there is some segregation for  different types of  modalities and different types of  variables as well so now we have this  population partially overlapping groups  of neurons that are encoding things and  they're still circulating information  much in the same way sharing information  to get better estimates and inferences  and  but now it's done at the population  level implicitly in some interacting  projections or interacting high  dimensional patterns of activity  so this becomes complicated how do we  even think about that  and at the same time you might ask well  there there could be multiple ways of  implementing the same computations  and that would be what what we often  call mechanism but there are many  mechanisms that can generate the same  computations  and so to some degree i would advocate  looking for the abstract qualities that  those mechanisms have in common rather  than focusing on the biophysical details  there are some cases where you want to  when you're dealing with interventions  you want to figure out which neurons to  zap or what pharmaceuticals to apply  but oftentimes to understand the  computational function it's good to take  this more abstract view so for example  here we might have a quadratic function  a square that we want to apply to some  information that's represented by a  population of neurons here  and you could implement that square by a  bunch of quadratic products of these  neurons  and then sum those together to get an  estimate but equivalently you can have a  bunch of  threshold linear units that you just add  together and if you uniformly distribute  their thresholds the result is again a  quadratic function  and so i would say in many cases it's  not super important which one of these  is happening in the brain if we want to  understand the computational function  now once we take that perspective where  we're really interested in the task  relevant aspects and this more abstract  property of the mechanism that's going  on then i think it's really helpful to  refer to this neural coding framework of  partially overlapping  processes of encoding recoding and  decoding  so encoding here  refers to how neurons relate to the  stimuli  and decoding is how the neurons derive  their behavior the behavior of the  animal recoding is all the stuff in the  middle where neurons are transforming  their their information  now oftentimes  the decoder is the the experimentalist  or the neuroscientist who's recoding the  neural activity here it's the brain  decoding its own activity and so it's  the same process it's just a different  thing doing the decoding and i think  that this breakdown of encoding recoding  decoding is very helpful  so when we're thinking about that  i like to think about  the task relevant projections or  coordinates in the manifold of neural  activity so neural activity is reduced  uh restricted to some low dimensional  sub subgroups or patterns which we  usually refer to as a manifold  but within that manifold there are  different coordinates some of which are  task relevant like the coordinates at  the top of this kind of smooshed blocks  and some of them are irrelevant like the  green coordinate here  so if we look at this orange line that  orange line is the dynamics of  particular set of task variables during  one task traces out some coordinates in  this belief space  and then orthogonal to that  are all the irrelevant directions and so  the set of irrelevant um  coordinates that all have the same task  relevant coordinates that traces out a  ribbon over time in this case a  one-dimensional  ribbon  and any trajectory within that ribbon  going up and down following the same  orange trajectory is going to give you  the same task performance so here you  see this black line wiggling back and  forth this might be the neural activity  at a population level wiggling around  all consistent with the same task  dynamics  and so 