fs so this is again a nice  success that you're able to interpret  the neural data in accordance with  this behavioral model and that the  neural dynamics explain or recapitulate  the behavior the inferred behavioral  dynamics or the the inferred latent  beliefs that motivate the behavior  and then finally you can look at the  behavior itself and say how often does  this agent pick different actions  according to the behavioral model and  according to the neural model and again  we get a good agreement here so this is  more encouraging um  suggestion that this type of approach  where you have a good behavioral model  for latent beliefs  or latent dynamics uh and the low  dimensional projection or low  dimensional manifold  that explains the task relevant space  that that can be really fruitful for  understanding real brains so let's see  well that's work for the future  but i'll just conclude here by saying in  summary we presented this framework of  inverse rational control which views the  brain as rational but not optimal and  that gives us theoretical guidance on  the kind of sub-optimality that you  would expect to see in the brain or that  that we can use to interpret the brain  now the mechanisms by which those  computations are performed there are  many  and they could be in lots of different  forms  but we can collapse many of them down to  equivalent computations  and i would say that that's very  satisfactory understanding of what's  going on in the brain  now there are really crucial questions  about generalization and some of these  equivalent computations that are  equivalent for one task may be quite  distinct for another task  and so then that would allow you to test  whether that mechanism is correct by  just looking at a generalization  performance which is a key  challenge in any model of the brain  but once you have this notion of  equivalences you can use dimensionality  reduction or other related techniques to  study the computational dynamics 